  "finalAnswerPreview": "## Answer\\n\\n**The integration should expect `InvalidCursorError` — Ordarium error code `\\\"INVALID_CURSOR\\\"` — thrown fail‑closed by the state change feed (`changes()`), and it should treat that rejection as a hard, non‑retryable, store‑global failure. That is Record A (\\\"global\\\"), not Record B (\\\"per‑consumer\\\").**\\n\\n### Why the two local records don't actually conflict — they are not co‑applicable\\n\\n`docs/engineering/09-error-contract-a.md` and `-b.md` both claim \\\"normative, current scope\\\", same version scope, no dates, no revision log — so on their face they look irreconcilable. But the proposition is about a **stored *observation* cursor**, and the pinned dependency tells us what that is:\\n\\n- `@ordarium/core` (`dist/src/state.d.ts`, `types.d.ts`) defines \\\"observation\\\" as the revisioned **state change feed**: `StateChangeFeed.changes()` — *\\\"Incremental observation of committed state revisions after a durable position (ORD‑BOOT‑0).\\\"*\\n- Its cursor is described as *\\\"Opaque, **filter‑independent** durable position of the last delivered change\\\"*, and explicitly contrasted with the other read surfaces: *\\\"**Unlike** the `nextCursor?` pagination of the other read surfaces,",
